  "content": "{\n  \"findings\": \"Assume H. We prove by strong induction on n that every positive integer n reaches 1 under the standard Collatz map. Base case: n=1 already equals 1. Inductive step: let n>1 and assume every positive integer m<n reaches 1. If n is even, then T(n)=n/2<n, so the induction hypothesis shows that n/2, and hence n, reaches 1. If n is odd, H supplies some k such that m=T^k(n) is positive and m<n. By the induction hypothesis, m reaches 1; appending that trajectory to the first k iterates from n shows that n reaches 1. Thus, conditional on H, every positive integer reaches 1. This proves only the implication H => the stated Collatz conclusion; it does not prove H itself.\",\n  \"claim_status\": \"SATISFIED\",\n  \"first_missing_step\": null,\n  \"unexpected_findings\": []\n}",
